WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:00.840
Instructor: So now that we've made it

00:00.840 --> 00:01.920
through our first commit,

00:01.920 --> 00:04.560
let's repeat the process and hopefully speed it up.

00:04.560 --> 00:06.840
We'll do some work, we'll add some changes

00:06.840 --> 00:08.910
and then we'll make a commit.

00:08.910 --> 00:11.670
So it's day two of writing my novel,

00:11.670 --> 00:13.590
or maybe it's afternoon of day one,

00:13.590 --> 00:14.940
and I've come back from lunch.

00:14.940 --> 00:16.200
I have my outline.

00:16.200 --> 00:18.810
I've flushed out the very basic characters,

00:18.810 --> 00:21.390
the four characters so far that I've included,

00:21.390 --> 00:24.330
and I'm gonna start working on chapter 1.

00:24.330 --> 00:27.690
So I'm gonna make chapter 1 just a text file.

00:27.690 --> 00:30.480
I'll just do it from within my text editor,

00:30.480 --> 00:31.710
but I could do it from the command line,

00:31.710 --> 00:32.810
it doesn't matter how,

00:33.660 --> 00:36.060
and I'm just gonna save that file.

00:36.060 --> 00:38.960
Maybe I'll put something in here like chapter 1.

00:38.960 --> 00:42.480
Hmm, I'm not sure what I'm gonna write in there.

00:42.480 --> 00:45.750
And of course, if I go over here to my terminal

00:45.750 --> 00:48.453
what do we expect to see when I type git status?

00:50.160 --> 00:52.620
Here it's telling me, as we've seen before,

00:52.620 --> 00:57.120
that there's an untracked file called chapter1.txt.

00:57.120 --> 01:00.570
Also, if I go into any of the other files,

01:00.570 --> 01:04.380
like how about characters.txt or maybe the outline

01:04.380 --> 01:06.180
and I type something else in here.

01:06.180 --> 01:08.490
Let's just change a single character.

01:08.490 --> 01:11.760
How about Jordan, I'm gonna make a lowercase J

01:11.760 --> 01:13.653
and I'm going to save this file.

01:15.240 --> 01:16.690
If I run git status

01:17.850 --> 01:19.860
now we see something new.

01:19.860 --> 01:24.390
It says there's a modified file, outline.txt.

01:24.390 --> 01:27.300
It doesn't matter that I just changed the case of a letter.

01:27.300 --> 01:31.320
I did change that file and git has been watching that file.

01:31.320 --> 01:34.240
Unlike chapter1, which is an untracked file,

01:34.240 --> 01:37.530
outline, it's already been added and committed previously,

01:37.530 --> 01:39.397
so git knows about it and it says,

01:39.397 --> 01:41.700
"Hey, you modified that file."

01:41.700 --> 01:43.650
So let's say I actually continue to work,

01:43.650 --> 01:46.110
though I'm not ready to do a commit or anything like that,

01:46.110 --> 01:47.640
Ah, what should I do?

01:47.640 --> 01:49.350
I'm gonna start on chapter 1.

01:49.350 --> 01:52.710
I'm gonna just copy some of the text from the Great Gatsby.

01:52.710 --> 01:56.760
This is all available these days online free and legally,

01:56.760 --> 02:00.000
so I'm just gonna take some of this chapter 1,

02:00.000 --> 02:01.920
if you really wanna follow along, you don't need to,

02:01.920 --> 02:04.950
but if you want to, I'll include this link,

02:04.950 --> 02:07.500
and then I'm just gonna paste this in my chapter 1.

02:08.520 --> 02:10.080
Okay?

02:10.080 --> 02:12.210
Simultaneously, I decide I need to do

02:12.210 --> 02:15.420
a bit more on the outline, so I'll add some here.

02:15.420 --> 02:16.253
There we go.

02:16.253 --> 02:18.600
Here's a bit more work that I've done on the outline

02:18.600 --> 02:21.240
to match some of the stuff that I've been working on

02:21.240 --> 02:23.280
in chapter 1 so far.

02:23.280 --> 02:26.820
Okay, so I'm gonna save both of those files.

02:26.820 --> 02:29.820
And then I'm gonna go over here again to my terminal,

02:29.820 --> 02:31.380
type git status,

02:31.380 --> 02:33.750
and we see basically the same thing.

02:33.750 --> 02:35.580
The files have changed.

02:35.580 --> 02:37.200
I mean, git's not telling me right now

02:37.200 --> 02:39.330
what has been modified in this file.

02:39.330 --> 02:41.580
There is a way for us to see exactly what's changed,

02:41.580 --> 02:43.200
but for now, all that it says

02:43.200 --> 02:45.330
is something changed in outline.

02:45.330 --> 02:47.820
So if I'm ready to make a commit, I have to decide,

02:47.820 --> 02:50.310
you know, am I going to group those files together?

02:50.310 --> 02:53.370
And I am because they both had to do with chapter 1

02:53.370 --> 02:55.140
and the work I did on chapter 1.

02:55.140 --> 02:58.920
So I'm going to add outline.txt,

02:58.920 --> 03:00.150
and I'll do it in one go.

03:00.150 --> 03:04.800
If I separate them by a space, I'll also add chapter1.txt.

03:04.800 --> 03:06.840
And now if I type git status,

03:06.840 --> 03:09.090
we see that whenever I do commit next,

03:09.090 --> 03:12.690
both of these changes, new file chapter1

03:12.690 --> 03:15.930
and the modifying, modification of outline,

03:15.930 --> 03:18.780
both of those will be included in the next commit.

03:18.780 --> 03:20.010
So I'm gonna commit,

03:20.010 --> 03:22.560
git commit -m, what's my message here?

03:22.560 --> 03:23.460
What did I do?

03:23.460 --> 03:27.300
How about "begin work on chapter 1".

03:27.300 --> 03:30.600
Okay? And type git status.

03:30.600 --> 03:32.160
Quick, what do you think we'll see?

03:32.160 --> 03:33.600
We saw it earlier.

03:33.600 --> 03:36.333
Nothing to commit, working tree clean.

03:37.218 --> 03:39.690
Okay, so the next thing I wanna talk about

03:39.690 --> 03:42.990
is a command called git log.

03:42.990 --> 03:45.480
And the git log command doesn't do anything,

03:45.480 --> 03:49.080
it's much like git status, it just retrieves information.

03:49.080 --> 03:50.910
And the information it retrieves to us

03:50.910 --> 03:55.380
is a log of the commits for a given repository.

03:55.380 --> 03:57.840
And in this repo we only have two commits.

03:57.840 --> 04:00.600
Now there's a bunch of stuff in here for each commit.

04:00.600 --> 04:02.460
Here is one commit,

04:02.460 --> 04:04.200
here is another.

04:04.200 --> 04:06.300
And for each one you can see the author,

04:06.300 --> 04:07.200
the dates,

04:07.200 --> 04:09.120
those, well the author and the email

04:09.120 --> 04:11.010
are things that you can configure,

04:11.010 --> 04:12.540
and we did configure early on,

04:12.540 --> 04:16.320
so it just uses whatever your local git has configured

04:16.320 --> 04:18.060
for username and email.

04:18.060 --> 04:19.380
Then we have the dates

04:19.380 --> 04:22.200
and then we have the commit message.

04:22.200 --> 04:24.930
And then up top, this crazy looking thing

04:24.930 --> 04:27.120
called the commit hash, we don't need to worry about for now

04:27.120 --> 04:30.120
but definitely we will come back and reference.

04:30.120 --> 04:32.310
And then here is the second commit.

04:32.310 --> 04:35.130
So at this point, honestly git log is just a way for us

04:35.130 --> 04:37.590
to demonstrate that when we ran git commit,

04:37.590 --> 04:41.010
it did something, and that's kind of it for now.

04:41.010 --> 04:44.100
So it's the next morning, I am very excited.

04:44.100 --> 04:47.160
I, I know how I want to finish chapter 1.

04:47.160 --> 04:50.850
So I'm going to start by just adding more to our outline.

04:50.850 --> 04:51.683
There we go.

04:51.683 --> 04:53.460
So I'll save again,

04:53.460 --> 04:54.510
if you're following along,

04:54.510 --> 04:56.340
you can just change a single character

04:56.340 --> 04:57.810
and git is going to notice.

04:57.810 --> 05:00.217
Now if I type git status, right, it tells us,

05:00.217 --> 05:02.370
"Hey, you modified that file again."

05:02.370 --> 05:05.730
Next up, now that I finished that outline for chapter 1,

05:05.730 --> 05:08.010
I'm gonna go back to the actual file

05:08.010 --> 05:10.050
and finish typing the chapter out,

05:10.050 --> 05:12.930
which again, I'm just copying from the real Great Gatsby.

05:12.930 --> 05:15.480
Okay, so I've got a lot more text in here.

05:15.480 --> 05:17.580
Just took it from the Great Gatsby website,

05:17.580 --> 05:19.650
and I'm gonna save.

05:19.650 --> 05:22.920
And now I finished chapter 1, I outlined it,

05:22.920 --> 05:26.400
and I'm ready to go through the same steps again and commit.

05:26.400 --> 05:28.275
So I always start with git status,

05:28.275 --> 05:30.570
and I'm gonna show you one other option

05:30.570 --> 05:33.540
for how I can add these two files simultaneously

05:33.540 --> 05:36.090
rather than having to add them by name.

05:36.090 --> 05:39.180
If I do git add followed by a dot,

05:39.180 --> 05:40.830
I actually have a slide on this.

05:40.830 --> 05:41.670
Here we are,

05:41.670 --> 05:45.900
git add dot will stage all changes at once.

05:45.900 --> 05:48.990
So whatever we have unstaged at the moment,

05:48.990 --> 05:50.460
they're not going to be committed,

05:50.460 --> 05:54.060
when I use git add dot, both in this case,

05:54.060 --> 05:56.190
but if I had 50 files that were modified,

05:56.190 --> 05:58.110
all 50 would be staged.

05:58.110 --> 06:00.210
And it can be useful but also if you've worked

06:00.210 --> 06:02.190
on a bunch of things and, you know,

06:02.190 --> 06:04.890
disparate features that aren't necessarily ready

06:04.890 --> 06:06.840
to all be committed at once,

06:06.840 --> 06:10.110
it's much better to be surgical when you make your commits.

06:10.110 --> 06:12.090
But for now, I finished chapter 1.

06:12.090 --> 06:15.420
The outline had to do with chapter 1, so I'll add both.

06:15.420 --> 06:17.100
If I type git status,

06:17.100 --> 06:19.980
we can see both of those have been modified.

06:19.980 --> 06:24.980
And now I'll commit, git commit -m "finish chapter 1".

06:25.470 --> 06:28.590
Great. Type git log just as proof.

06:28.590 --> 06:31.230
And there it is, finish chapter 1.

06:31.230 --> 06:36.230
Okay, so then I go to bed and I'm having a real rough time

06:36.300 --> 06:38.640
with how long chapter 1 is,

06:38.640 --> 06:41.460
and I'm thinking about it in at night as I'm in bed,

06:41.460 --> 06:43.260
I'm like, "Yeah, I really need to fix that

06:43.260 --> 06:45.990
in the next morning. I need to break this chapter up."

06:45.990 --> 06:47.760
So that's what I decide to do.

06:47.760 --> 06:51.600
I wake up and I move my outline around a bit

06:51.600 --> 06:53.910
to reflect my new changes.

06:53.910 --> 06:56.100
I decide that right here

06:56.100 --> 06:59.400
I want to split my outline.

06:59.400 --> 07:02.430
So I'm gonna add chapter 2

07:02.430 --> 07:04.950
now starts right here.

07:04.950 --> 07:07.200
So I'm just gonna move some content around.

07:07.200 --> 07:09.810
Of course, by doing that and saving the file,

07:09.810 --> 07:12.750
I've now dirtied our working tree,

07:12.750 --> 07:14.100
meaning that we have some changes

07:14.100 --> 07:15.690
that have not yet been committed.

07:15.690 --> 07:18.420
Then I'll go to the chapter 1 file.

07:18.420 --> 07:20.130
I'm gonna take half of this,

07:20.130 --> 07:21.300
the second half,

07:21.300 --> 07:23.070
and cut it out of here,

07:23.070 --> 07:24.360
save this file.

07:24.360 --> 07:26.550
And then I'm gonna make a chapter 2 file,

07:26.550 --> 07:31.260
chapter2.txt and I'm going to paste that in there and save.

07:31.260 --> 07:33.810
So now I have updated chapter 1,

07:33.810 --> 07:35.250
I've updated the outline,

07:35.250 --> 07:38.940
and I've created a new file, chapter 2.

07:38.940 --> 07:41.340
If I saved all three files, which I did,

07:41.340 --> 07:43.387
and I run git status, git tells us,

07:43.387 --> 07:45.600
"Hey, there's three things that changed."

07:45.600 --> 07:48.930
Really, two things that it knows for sure were modified

07:48.930 --> 07:53.370
and a third untracked file that it's aware of vaguely,

07:53.370 --> 07:56.790
it sees that it exists but it's not tracking the changes.

07:56.790 --> 07:58.050
So I'm gonna add all of them

07:58.050 --> 08:00.060
and we can use the same shortcut I showed you,

08:00.060 --> 08:01.200
git add dot,

08:01.200 --> 08:03.060
because they all have to do with the same thing,

08:03.060 --> 08:05.100
it's just the same piece of work.

08:05.100 --> 08:08.497
Something like when I commit my message will be, you know,

08:08.497 --> 08:13.497
"split chapter 1 into 2 pieces"

08:15.480 --> 08:17.460
or something like that.

08:17.460 --> 08:18.360
Looks good.

08:18.360 --> 08:21.480
Type git status, working tree clean,

08:21.480 --> 08:22.860
and git log.

08:22.860 --> 08:24.573
We see we have a new commit.
